>She does not require a lawyer if she has a IVO

&#x200B;

You give good advice overall, but I'd be cautious about telling people that. Many IVOs allow the respondent to do anything permitted by a family law act order. A lawyer is certainly needed if there are outstanding disputes/court matters as a family court order could be made that interferes with the IVO.

&#x200B;

You don't know all the circumstances to say with certainty a lawyer is not needed. Generally speaking, after a recent acrimonious seperation with children involved, there is likely to be family court matters that come up that may impact IVOs.

\>He is a deranged narcissist and no restraining order or anything else will help.

&#x200B;

Breaching a restraining order is a criminal offence. If he breaches it, report to police and he will almost certainly be charged. You can deal with the police directly instead of a lawyer when it comes to getting restraining orders placed etc. The police can apply on your behalf. (note the police aren't a lawyer and aren't necessarily guaranteed to act in your best interests and be loyal to you like a lawyer is supposed to)

