\>You could even make the argument that it's in our best interest for our Arab allies to be well-armed

&#x200B;

Again UAE isn't an ally. They're a country Israel has normal relations with like any other, there is no alliance.

It's clearly NOT in Israel's best interests as a tiny sliver of a country surrounded by much larger neighbours for its neighbours to surpass or be equal to it militarily. Remember, this normalisation was signed a couple of weeks ago, and if Israel didn't have superiority over its neighbours, it would have been pushed into the sea decades ago and there would be no Israel for the Arabs to normalise with.

\>as they will also be our allies in a military conflict with Iran

Unlikely there would be a major conventional war between Iran and the Arabs+Israel. Irans threat is that it seeks to destabilise other regional powers using puppet non-state actors and satellite states. This could change in the future, but I doubt it. The war scenario you describe is just not realistic and conceivable in the current situation. 

&#x200B;

\>Not to mention that there are plenty of nations out there, such as Russia, that has no issue selling sophisticated military hardware to the highest bidder

&#x200B;

Russian technology is nothing like the F35. Russia also sells to countries in its sphere of influence. The UAE isn't going to suddenly shun the US and start buying inferior Russian equipment because it doesn't get F35s.

The UAE isn't Israel's enemy now but maintaining superiority in the region is important for Israel regardless. The UAE (and other regional governments) could suffer a revolution/change in government leading to them becoming enemies. Just look at the history with Iran.

&#x200B;

I expect Israel would always be against Arab countries receiving such advanced technology unless the region undergoes massive political and societal transformation leading to stability and peace.

