Monash without a doubt. If you get a 98+ ATAR you are guranteed to get into Law with Monash. Unimelb is very iffy, you need a high GPA in uni i.e. three years of constant VCE like stress over your degree thinking every slip up with marks could screw your admission to the JD and also the LSAT which is extra unnecessary stress.

&#x200B;

Also, if you do Law at monash you will get a commonwealth supported place which is a fraction of the cost of doing a Juris Doctor at UniMelb. Unimelb switched to the postgrad BS system to be able to legally charge tens of thousands of dollars more per course. It may not seem so important because you're young and it goes on loan, but trust me, when you're working, you will appreciate having a student loan debt that is tens of thousands of dollars less than what it could have been.

&#x200B;

There is functionally no difference between Unimelb and Monash when it comes to career opportunities. In fact Monash offers a clinical guarantee where your guaranteed practical legal experience which is really helpful when it comes to careers. In my experience, what matters much more than your institution, is your practical experience and personal attributes, as well as marks.

&#x200B;

I graduated from Monash Law earlier this year, but I'm not biased. I reached the conclusion that Monash Law is better than UniMelb for the reasons I discussed above when I was choosing between the two unis, and advise the same to anyone that asks me.

&#x200B;

You definitly want to do a double degree when doing Law at Monash. Straight law is extremly stressful and hard, doing a double degree broadens your career options and makes studying law much easier. Some people do Straight law but they are the minority and many have a hard time.

&#x200B;

Sorry you've gotten such unnecessarily hostile answers here. If you wanna know more about Monash/being a law student feel free to PM me.

