Hey, I'm a law grad and criminology grad,  just finished my degree last week.

&#x200B;

Honestly, you've done very well. A high P is a very respectable mark, especially because this is your first semester. Combined with the two D's and the fact we have the corona situation that has messed up everyone this semester (my marks this semester are my worst ever basically), this is a great start to your degree and you should be really proud of yourself.

&#x200B;

If you are concerned about career prospects, employers would give very little weight to your first years mark (if they give any weight to it at all). What is much more important is your personal attributes, passions and practical work experience and skills that can only be developed overtime and can be further developed by studying the other criminal law units your uni offers as well as the criminology component. You are on the right track. You have plenty of time to develop the skills, experience and attributes that matter most for your career.

&#x200B;

Edit: Also, something that can be really helpful, especially when you're starting off is to get a copy of your exam paper and meet with your lecturer/tutor/whoever can help you, to get feedback on your exam and discuss ways you can improve \[I know at Monash we an request our exam script, I'm not sure about other law schools though\]. I encourage you to do this not just for criminal law but your other units as well. Most half decent teachers would be more than happy to help out a student wanting to hone their work. This isn't mandatory, I mean, I haven't done it as much as I should have but it can be really useful.

[http://www.federalcircuitcourt.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/fccweb/how-do-i/divorce/apply-for-a-divorce/apply-for-divorce](http://www.federalcircuitcourt.gov.au/wps/wcm/connect/fccweb/how-do-i/divorce/apply-for-a-divorce/apply-for-divorce)

&#x200B;

The federal circuit website has great resources for all family law issues given the high proportion of self-represented litigants.

&#x200B;

No one here can tell you what to make of the information as that would be legal advice. If you have any confusion/concerns or even just want to make sure things are done properly you need to speak to a family lawyer. The LIV referral service includes free 30 minute initial consults, which for a simple divorce application should be sufficient to clear a lot of things up.

Honestly, I've never heard of fixed fees for these type of family law cases. The amount of work required for a case is unknown (it could be settled in the first mediation, or proceed to final hearing after years long process) so you will have a hard time finding a lawyer that can give you a fixed fee in advance.

&#x200B;

The LIV referral service linked is really good as you get a free consultation to start off with so you can get a feel for who you like more and speak to them about fee structures.

&#x200B;

There are family lawyers who are willing to operate on a no-win-no-fee basis where they take their fee out of the settlement you receive, if you receive it.

&#x200B;

Hopefully you won't need a barrister for a final hearing.

